Original commit body from 0e92d7a097edd46c4afd4beb0b4aea720cccc059:

Asking `ci-debug logs` for a gate that did not run on a commit matched no
status, fell past the prefer-the-failed-gate rule (nothing had failed),
landed on statuses[0], and printed a DIFFERENT pipeline's transcript at
rc 0 with nothing marking the substitution.

Live on dev ea0d6a83ab, which carries only push/woodpecker:

  ci-debug logs cron/full-suite ea0d6a83ab   ->  "1556 passed"  rc 0

That is a cron gate reporting success on a commit it never ran on. Only
the test count (1556, not 22825) and a 16-second duration distinguished
it from an answer. The real cron gate last ran on 1de346d6d4 and is RED.

The fallback itself is right and stays -- WI-zavut's five pipeline
tests are untouched. What was missing is that the caller is never told.
Both selectors (pipeline and step) now report HOW they chose, and both
route the report through one _wp_warn_substituted rather than growing a
second copy of the rule.

The warning is narrow on purpose. ONE name is tried as a gate and then
as a step, so a fallback at either level alone is ordinary: a gate name
never matches a step, and a step name never matches a gate. Only when
NEITHER matches has the caller been handed something they did not ask
for. Warning on the common path would train the reader to skip it, so
the two quiet cases are pinned by their own tests.

Controls: both new tests fail with the fix reverted and pass with it;
the three quiet-path tests pass in both arms.

